append_to_row('F-BFC-03', ' <strong>Update 2026-11-21 (session 9bee7c49, after PR #39 merged):</strong> the published feed now holds <strong>19</strong> <code>KXBTCY-27JAN0100-B97500</code> rows, not 74 — the same one-swing-per-ticker rule applies. The oscillation itself is unchanged and remains an observed property of the demo API, not a claim that the response is erroneous; those rows still must not be treated as a validated ROI sample.')

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- new rows
NEW = '''        <tr><td><span id="F-60"></span>F-60</td><td>Repo irregularity (fixed 2026-11-21)</td><td><code>keepalive.yml</code> carried the header comment "The other two writers queue behind this one instead of racing it (see the shared group name in live-alerts.yml and refresh-data.yml)" — but the three workflows used three <em>different</em> <code>concurrency</code> groups (<code>writer-${{ github.ref }}</code>, <code>live-alerts</code>, <code>refresh-data-${{ github.ref }}</code>), so they did race. Measured 2026-11-21 by reading the three workflow files. The comment is now corrected. The writers no longer need to share a group, because publishing no longer merges (see <a href="#F-44">F-44</a>).</td><td>.github/workflows/</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/blob/main/.github/workflows/keepalive.yml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">keepalive.yml &rarr;</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/blob/main/.github/workflows/live-alerts.yml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">live-alerts.yml &rarr;</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/blob/main/.github/workflows/refresh-data.yml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">refresh-data.yml &rarr;</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed 2026-11-21</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-61"></span>F-61</td><td>Content defect (fixed 2026-11-21)</td><td>The published live feed carried <strong>99 alert rows, every one of them from the single ticker <code>KXPRESNOMD-28-AKLO</code></strong>, which flapped between $0.80 and $0.00 between 16:28 and 19:26 UTC on 2026-11-20. The two scrolling chat panes therefore carried exactly one event, repeated 99 times, and the high-ROI pane showed 18 copies of the same oscillation. The de-duplication rule now keeps one swing per ticker per 15 minutes, direction-independent, with the narrow 3-minute window applied only across detectors (candle vs trade vs sample describe the same move). The committed feed was also re-de-duplicated offline with the engine's own exported <code>collapseDuplicates()</code>: 99 rows &rarr; 12, which is the same count the live engine produced independently on its next run. Nothing was invented or re-priced — every surviving row is byte-for-byte the row the API produced, with its <code>source_url</code>. See <a href="#F-BFC-01">F-BFC-01</a> for the feed-versus-narrative conflict this exposed.</td><td>Live alert feeds, alerts/</td><td><a href="data/live/feed.json">the published feed</a> · <a href="docs/ALERTS.md">docs/ALERTS.md</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/blob/main/tools/live_engine.mjs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tools/live_engine.mjs &rarr;</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed 2026-11-21</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-62"></span>F-62</td><td>Test defect (fixed 2026-11-21)</td><td>The readability guard added to <code>scripts/check_alerts.mjs</code> by PR #39 was placed <em>after</em> the block that reports violations and exits, so its <code>fail()</code> only pushed onto an array that was never read again: the guard could not fail a run. Verified by running it against the 99-row flood it was written for — it <strong>passed</strong>. The guard was moved ahead of the report and rewritten as a fixed-point check (the published feed must be a fixed point of the engine's own <code>collapseDuplicates()</code>), so it now exits non-zero on that same feed and passes on the corrected one. A "more than half of the alerts come from one ticker" heuristic was tried first and rejected, because after the fix a genuinely active contract legitimately owns 12 of 12 rows over three hours, so that heuristic flags correct feeds.</td><td>CI, live alert feeds</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/blob/main/scripts/check_alerts.mjs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">scripts/check_alerts.mjs &rarr;</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/pull/39" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PR #39 &rarr;</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed 2026-11-21</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-63"></span>F-63</td><td>Feature defect (fixed 2026-11-21)</td><td>The "Resolved events — how each one settled" table could never fill. The engine kept a resolution only when the ticker's row was still in the open-market set, but a market leaves that set the moment it settles, so every resolution was dropped as "untracked". Measured on the live feed: <strong>500 settled markets seen, 0 recorded</strong> — and the official demo API does return them: <code>GET /markets?series_ticker=KXPGATOUR&amp;status=settled</code> answers with markets carrying <code>"result":"no"</code>, <code>settlement_value_dollars</code> and <code>settlement_ts</code>. The brief asks the dashboard to follow live events until they resolve and to show how each one resolved (yes or no), so this was a requirement gap, not a cosmetic one. The engine now keeps a <code>followed</code> registry of every ticker it has ever seen, <code>collectResolutions()</code> also queries the series of followed markets rather than only the open ones, and two selftest assertions cover the case that actually occurred. The published feed still shows 0 resolutions until the next engine run observes a settlement; that is shown as an empty state rather than filled in with a guess.</td><td>Live alert feeds, alerts/</td><td><a href="https://external-api.demo.kalshi.co/trade-api/v2/markets?series_ticker=KXPGATOUR&amp;status=settled&amp;limit=5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the settled-market response (live) &rarr;</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/market_settlement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Market Settlement &rarr;</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/market_lifecycle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Market Lifecycle &rarr;</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed 2026-11-21</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-64"></span>F-64</td><td>Repo irregularity (fixed 2026-11-21)</td><td>This page published the anchor <code>F-54</code> twice: one row about unreliable series-list filters and one about the schedule event, with different meanings, so <code>flagged.html#F-54</code> resolved to whichever row came first. The same duplicate was found and repaired independently by two sessions on 2026-11-21 — it is recorded here because the second finding is what proves the repair was checked rather than assumed: a duplicate-id scan over all 70 published HTML files now returns zero duplicates, and every numeric flag ID on this page is unique.</td><td>This page</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/blob/main/templates/flagged.tpl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">templates/flagged.tpl &rarr;</a> · <a href="verification/2026-11-21-4830ab32.md">the parallel session's record &rarr;</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Fixed 2026-11-21</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><span id="F-65"></span>F-65</span></td><td>Stale statements (corrected 2026-11-21)</td><td>Several statements published on this page no longer reproduced when they were re-checked against their live sources on 2026-11-21, and are corrected above rather than left standing: <a href="#F-45">F-45</a> and <a href="#F-58">F-58</a> said no <code>schedule</code>-triggered run had ever been recorded — six now are; <a href="#F-46">F-46</a> said <code>package-lock.json</code> carried the root name <code>KalshiDash1</code> and dead <code>playwright</code>/<code>puppeteer-core</code> entries — the lockfile was regenerated on 2026-11-20 and now matches <code>package.json</code> exactly; <a href="#F-57">F-57</a> said zero alerts had been recorded — 31 are. Each row keeps its original wording inside the corrected text so the change is auditable rather than silent.</td><td>This page</td><td><a href="https://api.github.com/repos/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/actions/runs?event=schedule" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the schedule-run list (live) &rarr;</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/KalshiDash1/blob/main/package-lock.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">package-lock.json &rarr;</a> · <a href="data/live/feed.json">data/live/feed.json &rarr;</a></td><td><span class="badge good">Corrected 2026-11-21</span></td></tr>
'''

out = []
for l in lines:
    if l.strip() == '</tbody>':
        out.append(NEW.rstrip('\n'))
    out.append(l)
lines = out
src = '\n'.join(lines)

# intro paragraph
old = 'The F-L4-01…F-L4-08 block is the L4 live re-verification record'
new = ('The F-L4-01…F-L4-08 block is the L4 live re-verification record')
if old in src:
    src = src.replace(
        old,
        old + '. <strong>F-60 to F-65</strong> are the 2026-11-21 integration pass: the alert flood that filled the chat with one event 99 times, the guard that could not fail, the resolutions that could never be recorded, the duplicate anchor, and the published statements that no longer reproduced when re-checked.',
        1)

io.open(P, 'w', encoding='utf8').write(src)

ids = re.findall(r'\sid="([^"]+)"', src)
dups = sorted({i for i in ids if ids.count(i) > 1})
print('rows written; duplicate ids:', dups if dups else 'none')
